counterfactuals.output:
The proposal aims to improve theoretical and experimental determinations of ground-state hyperfine splittings in hydrogen-like and lithium-like highly charged ions. By studying both charge states of the same isotope—and constructing combinations that cancel dominant nuclear-structure uncertainties—it seeks to isolate higher-order bound-state quantum electrodynamics (QED) effects.

Key goals include:

- achieving higher-precision measurements of hyperfine transition energies using advanced ion traps, storage rings, and laser spectroscopy;
- refining calculations of radiative, recoil, electron-correlation, finite-nuclear-size, and nuclear-polarization corrections;
- reducing uncertainties associated with nuclear magnetization, particularly the Bohr–Weisskopf effect;
- comparing hydrogen-like and lithium-like ions to test many-electron and screening corrections; and
- probing QED in the exceptionally strong electromagnetic fields generated by high-\(Z\) nuclei.

The resulting theory–experiment comparisons would provide stringent tests of bound-state QED beyond the weak-field regime, improve knowledge of nuclear magnetic structure, and potentially reveal limitations of current strong-field atomic theory or signs of physics beyond the Standard Model.
